---
description: Supermemory persistent memory tools — use them proactively
alwaysApply: true
---

You have access to Supermemory MCP tools for persistent memory across coding sessions:

- `supermemory_search`: Search before answering questions about previous work, bugs, or patterns
- `supermemory_add`: Save decisions, fixes, preferences when user asks or when clearly valuable
- `supermemory_profile`: Get aggregated user profile and preferences
- `supermemory_list`: List recent memories for this project
- `supermemory_forget`: Delete a specific memory by ID
- `supermemory_get_config`: Returns `recallFilePath` for this session when `conversationId` is provided, or a single unambiguous recent path otherwise

Per-turn recall (read before other tool calls):
- Each composer session has its own recall file at `~/.cursor/.supermemory/recall/{projectTag}/{conversationId}/current-recall.md` (outside the project tree)
- At turn start, resolve the path from session context (`Supermemory recall file for this session: ...`), from the `Supermemory recall file:` line in injected recall blocks, or via `supermemory_get_config({ conversationId })` when you know the conversation id
- Read that absolute path before other tool calls if the file exists and is non-empty; use it silently when helpful
- Mid-turn, the file may be refreshed — re-read the same session path if topical recall may have updated (the path is stable for the session; content changes)
- Do not mention the file or recall pipeline unless the user asks

When to use MCP tools:
- User mentions past sessions, previous work, or "last time" → search first
- User says "remember", "save this", "don't forget" → add immediately
- After solving a hard bug → save the solution and root cause
- Discovering architectural patterns or conventions → save them
- User asks why something was done a certain way → search for context

Scope: use `project` for codebase-specific knowledge, `user` for cross-project preferences.
